# Something for you in Russian: кое-что, что-то, что-нибудь
subtitle: "Кое-что, что-то or что-нибудь: say I have something for you, ask if anyone called, and use them in every case."
tags: ["russian","кое-что","что-то что-нибудь","russian pronouns","russian grammar"]
teaches: ["grammar/indefinite-pronouns","vocabulary/holidays"]
thumb: "band \"Something for you in Russian\""
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | Tom, a warm, easy-going English speaker who knows Russian well; friendly with Masha, clear and unhurried; says Russian words the way a native Russian speaker would","b":"gemini:Leda | a friendly young Russian woman from Moscow, natural and lively, speaks English with a light Russian accent and says Russian words natively; never exaggerated"}
next: {"title":"With fifty-two guests in Russian: numbers in every case","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/russian-from-zero/164-russian-numerals-cases.cast"}

